## ABSTRACT
Zn-air batteries (ZABs) as a class of promising energy storage setups are generally powered by efficient and robust catalysts at the oxygen-involving cathode. Although the existing non-noble catalysts have outperformed noble Pt benchmark in the alkaline liquid-state ZABs, to the best of our knowledge few have excelled Pt in quasi-solid-state (QSS) ZABs. Herein, we found that an integrated  Mn-Co  cathode  derived  from  the  bimetallic  Mn/Co  metal  organic  frameworks  generates  a  1.4-fold  greater  power density in the QSS ZABs than a Pt cathode while its power density in liquid-state ZABs is only 0.8-fold of the latter. Moreover, such  Mn-Co  catalyst  delivers  high-rate  oxygen  reduction  reaction  (ORR)  capability  with  half-wave  potential  of $0.84\mathrm{~V~}$ .  The  indepth characterizations and analyses have demonstrated that the Co and Mn species show the specific affinity towards ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , respectively, synergizing the ORR process in the water-deficient environment of QSS ZABs. This work has enlightened the rational design of non-noble metal catalysts to improve the power density of QSS ZABs.
## KEYWORDS
non-noble metal catalysts, zinc-air battery, quasi-solid-state, synergistic effect
## Introduction
Quasi-solid-state $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air  batteries  (QSS  ZABs)  are  promising portable  electrochemical  energy  storage  devices  with  the advantages  of  high  theoretical  energy  density,  low  cost, environmental friendliness [1], excellent mechanical strength and flexibility  [2].  However,  QSS  ZABs  are  facing  the  challenge  of unsatisfying achievable power density, which originates from the sluggish kinetics of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in the QSS battery system [3]. In recent years, transition metal (Fe, Co, Ni) anchored  on  N-doped  carbon-based  materials  have  attracted much attention as multi-functional catalysts benefiting from their highly active metal-N-C (M-N-C) centers in the liquid electrolyte [4, 5].  Previous  studies  have  collectively  suggested  that $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{x}$ moieties  (especially $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}})$ have  Pt-like  catalytic  behaviour  in adsorption  and  dissociative  mechanism  of  alkaline  ORR  [6, 7]. However,  the  remarkable  lower  power  density  than  Pt-based catalysts  in  QSS  ZABs  has  become  the  biggest  barrier  in  their practical applications [8−10]. Currently, the screening of QSS ZAB catalysts is generally based on the rotating disk electrode (RDE) system [11−13]. It should be worthy noted that there is subversive difference  (typically  i.e.  humidity)  at  the  three-phase  interface among  the  catalysts, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under  the  QSS  and  RDE conditions (liquid electrolyte), respectively [14]. Since $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the alkaline ZABs not only facilitates ion transport, but also directly participates  in  the  ORR  at  the  cathode  [15−17].  For  example, Zhuang et al. identified a poorer activity on Mn-Co spinel catalyst than $\mathrm{Pt}$ in RDE test but a superior power in fuel cell tests [18]. Therefore, the catalyst with the performance below Pt in the RDE system  can  unsurprisingly  outperfrom  Pt  in  QSS  system  where mechanism  understanding  in  association  with  rational  catalyst design is still highly required.
Here,  we  unveil  that  by  pyrolyzing  the  as-synthesized  Mnincorporated  Co-zeolitic  imidazolate  framework  (ZIF)  precursor $\mathrm{{\langleMn/Co}}$ -ZIF) into Mn-decorated ultrafine Co nanoparticles (NPs) encapsulated  in  B  and  N  co-doped  carbon  (Mn/Co-BNC),  the $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC delivers a higher QSS power density but lower liquidstate  power  density  than  Pt.  The  effect  of  Mn  addition  on  the microstructural  characteristics  and  electrochemical  performance of $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ are  systematically  investigated  for  mechanism understanding behind the performance reversal in liquid-state and QSS  ZABs.  Specifically,  the  average  size  of  Co  NPs  in  the synthesized catalyst can be reduced to $\sim2\:\mathrm{nm}$ by Mn incorporated into Co crystal lattice with excellent dispersion and high loading. Impressively, $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC assembled into QSS ZAB offers higher power density up to $18\mathrm{\mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is 1.4 times greater than that  of  commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}.$ .  We  also  perform  the  comprehensive characterizations and analyses to identify an uncommon synergy between Mn and Co species under the QSS conditions where Mn species  favor $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ binding  but  the  Co  species  promote $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ activation. As a result, the synergistic effect in the Mn-Co catalyst is  pivotal  to  power  the  QSS  ZABs  where  water  is  severely insufficient  yet  necessary,  especially  to  produce  a  larger  power density.
