To  ensure  the  structural  integrity  after  the $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ions abundantly  incorporated  into  Co-based  ZIFs,  we  use  sodium borohydride $\mathrm{(NaBH_{4})}$ as the reductant in the synthesis of $\mathrm{Mn/Co}.$ - ZIF  to  create $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ voids  that  can  be  filled  with  Mn  ions  [19]. Moreover, in order to rule out the influence of B, we also prepare pure  ZIF  samples  with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ reductant,  denoted  as  Co-ZIF. Powder  X-ray  diffraction  (PXRD)  patterns  confirm  that  the structure  of  Co-ZIF  remains  unchanged  after  incorporating  Mn species,  which  are  isostructural  to  ZIF-67  (Fig. S3  in  the  ESM). The  addition  of $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ did  not  destroy  the  ZIFs configuration,  and  there  are  no  diffraction  peaks  belonging  to metallic Co and Mn. Figures S4 and S5 in the ESM illustrate the scanning  electron  microscopy  (SEM)  images  of  the  as-prepared ZIF  particles.  Both $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF  and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn/Co}}$ -ZIF  are  monodispersed polyhedrons but the average size of ZIF particles sharply decreases from $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ( $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF) to $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{{Mn/Co}}$ -ZIF). The metal contents are then determined by inductively coupled plasma (ICP) where the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn/Co}}$ ratio is 1.16:1 (Fig. S6 in the ESM), which is in good agreement with reaction ratio $\left(\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}=1:1\right)$ ).
To  clarify  the  role  of  Mn  sites  in $\mathrm{Mn}/{C_{0}}$ -BNC,  the  contrast sample derived from $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF (denoted Co-BNC) is also assessed using  RDE  method,  which  displays  a  very  poor $E_{1/2}$ of $0.67~\mathrm{V},$ implying its inferior ORR activity [20]. After Mn incorporating, the  ORR  activity  of  obtained $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ is  obviously  boosted (Fig. S7(a) in the ESM). According to the Koutechy–Levich (K–L) equation  [21], $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ is  also  an  excellent $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ORR electrocatalyst $\left(n=\sim4\right)$ , while Co-BNC shows a mixed $2e^{-}$ and $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ORR  pathway $(n=2.88)$ (Figs.  S6(b)  and  S7  in  the  ESM).  As shown  in Fig. S9  in  the  ESM,  the $\Delta E$ of $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ is  only 0.81  V $(\Delta E=E_{\mathrm{onset}}-E_{1/2},E_{\mathrm{onset}}$ :  the  onset  potential  in  oxygen evolution reaction (OER), $E_{1/2}$ : half-wave potential in ORR), which is smaller than Co-BNC (0.87 V), suggesting a better bifuntional oxygen (ORR/OER) catalysis. The assembled rechargeable liquidstate ZAB also exhibits a super narrow voltage gap (0.53 V), which is far better than that of Co-BNC ( $\mathrm{0}.73\mathrm{~V~}$ , Fig. S10 in the ESM). These  results  indicate  that  the  significant  improvement  of bifunctional catalytic activity emanates from the synergistic effects of Mn in rich-water system.
The morphology of Co-BNC is furtherly characterized by SEM images, no individual particles can be seen after pyrolysis (Fig. S11 in the ESM). Instead, wrinkled surfaces and abundant enlarged Co NPs ranging from 50 to $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ can be seen in the reconstructed irregular carbon substrate (Fig. S12 in the ESM). By contrast, the incorporation  of  Mn  stabilizes  ZIF-deritives  against  structure collapse, the $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ well inherits a regular morphology of Mn/Co-ZIF precursor under the bright-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) observation (Fig. 2(a)). The microstructure of $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ is further revealed by highangle annular dark-field STEM (HAADF-STEM) imaging.
The dark-field STEM (Fig. 2(b)) discloses that such particles are composed with ultrafine Co NPs wrapped in carbon frameworks. Additionally,  statistical  analysis  indicates  the  Co  NPs  have  an average diameter of $\sim2\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. 2(c) and $2(\mathrm{d})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). Because STEM is more sensitive to heavy elements [22], the high-resolution darkfield STEM image discloses brighter stripes of Co NPs with lattice orientation. Especially, compared with the regular Co (111) facet reported  in  previous  studies  (2.04  Å)  [23, 24],  the  interplanar spacing of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ (111) facet in $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ increases by \~ $11.7\%$ $({\bar{2}}.28{\bar{\mathrm{A}}},{}$ see Fig. 2(e)), which suggests that the incorporation of Mn effectively suppressed the growth of Co NPs. Selected area electron diffraction image further confirms that the crystal facets of (111) and  (200)  are  dominant  (Fig. 2(f))  [25].  Due  to  elemental  redistribution  in  the  pyrolysis,  Co  and  Mn  tend  to  distribute consistently and C, N, B appears simultaneously in ${\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}},$ implies that Co NP is Mn-incorporated and carbon skeleton is B and N co-doped, respectively (Figs. $2(\mathrm{h}){-}2(\mathrm{l}),$ ).
PXRD pattern reveals that the phase transition from precursors to products. After pyrolysis, all the products contain broad peak of C (002) and strong Co (111), (200), (220) diffraction peaks (Fig. 3(a))  [25].  Notably,  the  diffraction  peaks  of  Co  in $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ significantly  broaden  compared  with $\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC,  indicating  the smaller  Co  NPs.  Raman  spectroscopy  provides  chemical fingerprint for all the samples. As shown in Fig. 3(b), doping with B  results  in  an  increase  of  the  intensity  of  the $\mathrm{{Mn-N}}$ and ${\mathrm{Co-Co/Co-Mn}}$ peak in $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ (the peak with Raman shift of $688{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ cannot be distinguished from Co–Co to Co–Mn) [26]. The intensity ratio of D to G bands $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ decreases from 1.20 (Co-BNC)  to  0.70 $\left(\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co-BNC}\right)$ ,  indicating  the  catalyst  has higher degree of graphitization [27].
The surface chemical environments of the prepared samples are analysed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The full XPS image of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn}/C o$ -BNC suggests the existence of Co, Mn, B, N, O and C (Fig. 3(c)). The high-resolution C 1s spectra (Figs. S13 and S14  in  the  ESM)  can  be  fitted  to  six  component  peaks corresponding to B–C $(284.5\mathrm{eV})$ , C–C $(284.8~\mathrm{eV})$ ), C–N/C–B–O (285.3  eV), $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ (286.1  eV), $0-C=0$ $(287.4~\mathrm{~eV})$ and $C{=}C$ $(290.5~\mathrm{eV})$ [28]. The deconvoluted B 1s spectra assigned to three peaks can be interpreted as B–N ( $\mathrm{190.2~eV})$ , B–C $[191.3\mathrm{eV})$ and $-\mathrm{BC}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(192.4~\mathrm{~eV})$ ,  respectively  (Fig. S15  in  the  ESM)  [29], indicating  the  B,  N-dopant  in  carbon  to  form  BNC  ternary system, which is in good agreement with the energy dispersive Xray spectroscopy (EDS) results. The Mn $2\mathrm{p}$ spectra is identified to be  Mn–Mn  (640.6  eV),  Mn–N/Mn–B  (641.5  eV),  Mn–O $(642.4\mathrm{eV})$ and Mn–O $(643.6\mathrm{eV})$ , respectively (see Fig. 3(d)) [30]. The peaks in N 1s at 398.2, 400.1 and $401.4\mathrm{-}401.6\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to  B–N,  pyrrolic-N  and  graphitic-N,  respectively  [31, 32]. Graphite N has notable advantages in improving the conductivity of carbon-based materials [33]. The peak at $399.0\mathrm{eV}$ in N 1s (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S16 in the ESM), implies that the Mn incorporation leads  to  a  distinct  increase  in  the  active $\mathrm{Co{-}N/M n{-}N}$ content compared  to  the  Mn-free  sample  (Co-BNC).  In  the  highresolution XPS spectra of Co 2p shown in Fig. 3(f) the $\mathrm{Mn/Co^{-}}$ BNC exhibited three main peaks located at 778.6, 780.4 and 782.4 $\mathrm{^\circv}$ that could be attributed to Co–B/Co–N, Co–O and metallic Co [34], while a significant peak shift was observed $(0.3\mathrm{-}0.6~\mathrm{eV})$ 1 compared to the Co-BNC, the peaks shift to lesser binding energy may due to Mn addition decreases the cobalt oxidation states [35].
To  gain  insights  into  the  individual  functionality  of  the synergistic  Mn  and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ species  in  ZIF-derivatives,  we  have regulated  the  proportions  of  Mn  and  Co,  and  investigated  the products after pyrolysis (Table S1 in the ESM). To distinguish the samples by metal ratio $\left({\mathrm{Mn/Co}}\right)$ , the ZIF-derivatives are named as Mn/Co-BNC  (2:1), $\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}$ (1:1)  and  Mn/Co-BNC  (1:2). The electrochemical activity of all the samples is evaluated by the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and LSV in liquid electrolyte. The CV results show the same position of the reduction peak at $0.77\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, manifesting that the metal ratio cannot obviously affect the liquid-state oxygen reduction without rotating condition (Fig. S17 in the ESM). The Mn/Co-BNC (1:1) expresses a remarkable ORR activity where its $E_{1/2}$ is $60~\mathrm{mV}$ more than that of Mn/Co-BNC (2:1) and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn/Co}}$ -BNC (1:2) (Fig. 4(a)). Their OER performance is dramatically  comparable,  with  the  near  onset  potentials,  which means  that  there  is  no  difference  when  charging  Mn-Co-based ZABs. Subsequently, the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{K-L}$ plots based on LSV (Fig. S18 in the ESM) curves also confirm the $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ reaction pathway on all the ZIFderived electrodes [21]. Collectively, the effects of metal ratio on the ORR and OER performance are inconspicuous based on the RDE  test.  However,  the  inconsistency  between  RDE  results  and the charge–discharge curve of the assembled liquid-state batteries has  been  clearly  unveiled.  Rechargeable  liquid-state  ZABs  based on  the  Mn/Co-BNC  (1:1)  exhibit  a  highest  voltage  efficiency  of $77\%$ compared with those (respective $63\%$ and $61\%$ ) of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn/Co^{.}}$ BNC (2:1) and Mn/Co-BNC (1:2) (see insets of Fig. 4(a)).
Samples are then characterized with XPS to identify the surface oxygen  contents  (SOCs)  and  containing  species  for  synergistic mechanism understanding. The SOCs of Co-BNC (7.78 at.%) are comparable to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $6.98~\mathrm{at.\%}$ , Fig. S19 in the ESM). After Mn  doping,  the  SOCs  on  Mn/Co-BNC  (1:1)  are  significantly increased up to $43.86\mathrm{at.\%}$ . The comparison of the SOCs for all the catalysts  show  the  trend  of $\mathrm{SOC}_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC(1:1)}}$ (43.86  at. $\%$ $>$ $\mathrm{SOC}_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC(2:1)}}$ $(26.81\ \mathrm{at.\%})>\mathrm{SOC_{Mn/Co-BNC(1:2)}}$ $(24.78~\mathrm{at.\%})$ [36, 37],  which  confirms  the  optimization  of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn}/C o$ ratio  in  the precursors can effectively increase the SOCs. By distinct chemical shifts, the $\mathrm{O}_{2(\mathrm{ads})},\mathrm{OH}_{(\mathrm{ads})},\mathrm{OHO}_{(\mathrm{ads})}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{(ads)}}$ are identified (see Fig. $4(\mathrm{b}),$ ). A gradient decrease of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{(ads)}}$ content is achieved by the  increase  of  Mn  doping,  seeing  that  the $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ (1:2), $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC  (1:1)  and $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ (2:1)  exhibits $22.8\ \mathrm{at.\%},$ $21.1\ \mathrm{at.\%}$ , and $17.3~\mathrm{at.\%}$ , respectively (see Fig. $4(\mathrm{c}))$ , and the effect is reversed for $\mathrm{O}_{2(\mathrm{ads})}$ . Based on the SEM images of $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ with different metal ratios, there is a delicate balance between Co and Mn contributions where the size of Co NPs sharply decreases from 100 to $2\mathrm{nm}$ with the ratio of Mn and Co increased from 1:2 to 1:1 (see Fig. S20 in the ESM). So how can the $\mathrm{O}_{2(\mathrm{ads})}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{(ads)}}$ content have remained at a high level on the Mn/Co-BNC (1:1)? From the results, the $\mathrm{O}_{2(\mathrm{ads})}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{(ads)}}$ content are closely related to the size of Co NPs. The appropriate amount of Mn contributes to suppress the growth of ZIF and Co NP size. which might be because of the strong interaction between Co and Mn. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ melts and further volatilizes at $610~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [38], thus acting as barrier in the synthesis to the growth of Co NPs, which further determines the dispersion of exposed Mn and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ species of the catalysts.
The  proposed  synergistic  mechanism  of  the  Mn/Co-BNC catalyzed  ORR  is  then  illustrated  stepwise  based  on  the  above analyses  (see Fig. $4(\mathrm{d})_{,}^{.}$ ).  Assuming  that  Mn-OH  and $_{\mathrm{Co-OH}}$ represent the initial states, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is preferentially bound to the Mn site to yield $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn=O}_{2},$ along with a $1\mathrm{e}^{-}$ reduction to produce OH−. $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is preferentially bound to the ${\mathrm{Co}}$ site, as ${\mathrm{Co-OH}}_{2},$ also with a $1\mathrm{e^{-}}$ reduction to generate OH−. A surface proton transfer (Reaction (I) in Fig. 4(d)) can then occur from the $\mathrm{Co-OH}_{2}$ to the proximate $\mathrm{Mn-O}_{2},$ leading to a regenerated $_{\mathrm{Co-OH}}$ and a Mn-OOH species that is followed by a $1\mathrm{e}^{-}$ reduction to produce $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn=O}}$ and OH−. The $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Mn=O}$ can take the second proton, transferred from ${\mathrm{Co-OH}}_{2},$ to  regenerate  the  Mn-OH  (Reaction  (II)  in Fig. 4(d)).  The extraordinary  feature  of  this  mechanism  includes  the  proton mediation  by  the  turnover  of $\mathrm{Co-OH/Co-OH_{2}}$ and  the  surface proton  transfer  between  the  proximate $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ and  Mn  sites.  We believe that these energetic features are fundamental to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ activation and proton-transferred reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the $\mathrm{Mn/Co^{-}}$ BNC.
The superior electrocatalytic performances of Mn/Co-BNC in QSS  ZABs  have  been  testified  in  our  experimental  and characterization  results.  We  believe  that  this  exceptional phenomenon is because of the following reasons. The first origin is  the  unique  interactions  and  spatial  confinement  between  the Mn  ions  and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ ions  in  the  ZIF  precursor.  This  confinement effect inhibits the Co agglomeration during pyrolysis and mitigates the  structural  collapse  of  Mn/Co-ZIF,  greatly  improving  the dispersibility  of  Co  NPs  in  Mn/Co-BNC.  The  second  reason  is that the higher amount of Co NPs and degree of graphitization can facilitate the fast electron transport [39, 40]. The third factor is the introduction of Mn not only offers more active sites but also improves  surface  nitrogen  and  oxygen  contents.  The  nitrogencontaining groups in $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC acting as electrochemical active centers  can  increase  electronic  conductivity,  and  improve  the kinetics of redox reactions include metal-N-C [6, 41] and pyrrolicN  [42].  Meanwhile,  by  analyzing  metal $\left({\mathrm{Mn/Co}}\right)$ sites  and  their relevance with SOCs, finally we can make the conclusion that MnN-C and Co-N-C endow $_{\mathrm{Mn/Co-BNC}}$ differential affinity to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}},$ , which is the key factor for the power density of $\mathrm{Mn/Co^{-}}$ BNC-based QSS ZAB better than that of liquid-state ZAB.
## 3    Conclusion
In summary, we have reported an abnormal Mn-Co ORR catalyst with  strong  synergistic  effect  derived  from  Mn-doped $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIFs. We find the Mn doping can govern the growth and size of the Co NPs. By optimizing the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn/Co}}$ ratio in the catalyst, the Co NPs can be controlled at $2\mathrm{nm}$ and uniformly distributed in derivatives. Remarkably, the resultant catalyst has become more active in QSS than that in liquid-state ZABs. Its power density is about 1.4 times superior  to $20\%$ commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ -assembled  QSS  ZAB.  The enhanced catalytic performance is attributed to synergistic effect of Mn and Co species, which contribute to boost the power density of QSS batteries typically with low $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ content. This work not only  offers  a  practical,  high-power,  non-noble  catalyst  for  QSS ZABs, but also demonstrates a synergistic catalyst design strategy for practical QSS ORR.
## 4    Experimental section
## 4.1    Chemicals
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{\langleCo(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O_{3}}$ ≥ $99.0\%$ ), manganese chloride $(\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\quad\geq\quad99.0\%)$ and 2- methylimidazole $\left(2{\cdot}\mathrm{mIm};\ 98.0\%\right)$ were  purchased  from  SigmaAldrich $\mathrm{Co}.$ ., Ltd. and used without further purification. Sodium borohydride $(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}},$ $98.0\%$ )  was  purchased  from  China Medicine Shanghai Chemical Reagent Corporation. The deionized (DI) water with a resistivity of $18.25\mathrm{M}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ used throughout the experiments,  was  puried  with  a  water  ultrapure  cation  system (Milli-Q Advantage A10, Merck Millipore, USA).
## 4.2    Catalyst preparation
In  a  typical  synthesis  of $\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Co}$ -BNC  catalyst, $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $\left(0.588\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ and $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.4\ \mathrm{g)}$ were  dissolved  in  DI  water $(40\ \mathrm{mL})$ )  under  stirring  for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ to  obtain  solution  A.  We prepared  solution  B  by  adding  2-mIm  (2.627  g)  and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ $(0.234~\mathrm{g})$ in DI water $\mathrm{\langle40~mL\rangle}$ mixing for $15~\mathrm{min}$ , the solution B was then poured into the solution A under stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The reaction takes place in an open beaker, because of the initial stages of  the  reaction  produce  large  amounts  of  hydrogen.  When  no bubbles were produced, the reaction was continued in the sealed reaction  system.  The  resulting  products  were  collected  by centrifugal  separation  and  washed  three  times  in  DI  water  then dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12{\mathrm{h}}.$ . The as-prepared $\mathrm{Mn/Co-ZIF}$ was enclosed in a tube furnace under a stream of $5\%\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar},$ heated to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for $2\mathrm{h}.$ . In the end, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mn/Co}}$ -BNC was obtained upon cooling down to room temperature.
